title: Performance testing
tags: Development
kind: reference
audience: dev
aliases: performance perf
summary: Two layers for two kinds of regression — cost assertions that count the keys the store reads, in every test run, and a timed suite compared against its own recorded history, run on purpose.

metafolder has had the same failure several times: a change that is correct, and
passes every test, makes an operation ten times slower, and nobody notices until
the interface feels wrong weeks later — one query per metarecord on an index build,
one write per transaction in the watcher's flush ([[Slow log]] was born of that
one), a whole-forest rebuild on every `tree_ref` write, one HTTP request per
metarecord in `mf sync plan`, a process per entry in the shipped scripts
([[Writing a GUI script]]). Each was found afterwards, by a user.

The numbers are therefore taken on purpose, kept, and compared with what they were.
This is neither a metrics system nor a profiler: the slow log already answers
"where did the time go in ''this'' run". The question here is "is this slower than
it was last month, and by how much".

A regression comes in two shapes, and a clock catches only one.

!! Cost assertions — the shape of the work

An algorithmic regression changes ''how much work'' an operation does: a read per
row instead of one, a scan where a seek was enough, a walk over the whole log for a
bounded window. A clock is the wrong instrument for that — an assertion tight enough
to catch a threefold slowdown fails on a loaded machine
([[Tests that fail under load]]).

So this layer measures a ''counter'': the number of keys the store reads, for a
query and for a write alike (`KvStore::reads()`). It is exact, reproducible and the
same on every machine, so it runs in `cargo test` and in `scripts/check.sh`, and a
regression fails like any broken test.

```
cargo test -p metafolder-daemon --test kv_cost --test log_cost
```

One assertion carries the layer, ''growth invariance'': the same operation runs on
a repository and on a much larger one, and a bounded operation must not read more
on the larger. A count that grows with the data is one read per element, or a scan —
caught on a few thousand rows, in under a second.

|!File |!Holds |
|`crates/daemon/tests/log_cost.rs` |the log: a window of fifty operations, a window of twenty revisions, one metarecord read, one trim, one navigation step — the ''same'' number of keys on a log sixteen times longer, or a target further away ([[Reading the log costs the window]], [[What undoing used to cost]]) |
|`crates/daemon/tests/kv_cost.rs` |queries: a rare value, a sorted page, a text search, a subtree, the finder's search, combined operands, a path comparison — on 2 000 and 16 000 files, the same answer for at most half as many keys again ([[Sorting and postings]], [[Text search in the store]], [[The forest in the store]]) |
|`crates/daemon/tests/executor.rs` |the watcher's flush: the same arrivals cost the same in a repository eight times larger |
|`crates/daemon/tests/query_interrupt.rs`, `kv_postings.rs` |an abandoned query stops reading; a posting is read instead of its rows |

Three rules for writing one:

* ''The data must be larger than the bound.'' On a log shorter than the window, the whole log ''is'' the window, and the two sizes differ for the right reason.
* ''Go through the route's own preparation.'' A cost test that evaluates a query without the forest rewrite the route applies stayed green while the real request walked the whole forest ([[Storage pitfalls]]).
* ''Combine operands.'' Single-operand queries never exercised what a real search does; the combined shapes found that an equality read one key per holder.

!! Timed benchmarks — the size of the work

What remains needs a clock — a constant factor that doubled — and a clock needs a
baseline. [[The timed regression suite]] runs a fixed list of scenarios on
generated repositories, records each number with its machine, profile and commit,
and compares the run to the history of the same machine:

```
scripts/bench.sh
```

It takes minutes, so it is run on purpose, before and after a change that could
plausibly cost something.

!! What is deliberately not here

* ''No gate on timings in `check.sh`.'' A machine-dependent measurement that fails a build teaches people to ignore failing builds.
* ''No percentiles, no sampling, no aggregation across machines.'' The history is a list of numbers taken the same way on the same machine.
* ''No absolute target.'' "Under 100 ms" is a product decision, which belongs to the feature's own note; the suite only compares a measurement to its own past.

!! Other instruments

* [[Slow log]] and [[Diagnosing a slow operation]] — where the time of one real operation went, by phase; `mf slow replay` re-runs a logged query for its phases now.
* [[Benchmarks]] — the daemon and the GUI on real folders, and the per-phase timings of the panels.
* [[Storage pitfalls]] — reading `/proc` when a daemon seems stuck.
